                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mn 8
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_8
                    Hopf algebra
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#generalized_kacpaljutkin_algebras"} Generalized Kac-Paljutkin algebras
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em Hopf algebra
            is an abstraction of the properties of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
                of a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
                on a finite group, and more generally, the algebra of regular functions on an affine algebraic
                = succeed "-group;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+enveloping+algebra"} universal enveloping algebra
                of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
          %p
            where not only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            structure is remembered, but also the natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebra
            structure, making it a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra"} bialgebra
            = succeed "-operation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antipode"} antipode
          %p
            More intrinsically, a Hopf algebra structure on an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            is precisely the structure such as to make its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid monoidal category
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+functor"} fiber functor
            – this is the statement of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            for Hopf algebras.
          %p
            Hopf algebras and their generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroids"} Hopf algebroids
            arise notably as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+algebras"} groupoid convolution algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+Hopf+algebras"} combinatorial Hopf algebras
          %p
            There is a wide variety of variations of the notion of Hopf algebra, relaxing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/properties"} properties
            or adding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+Hopf+algebras"} weak Hopf algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-Hopf+algebras"} quasi-Hopf algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-triangular+Hopf+algebra"}> quasi
                \-)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangular+Hopf+algebras"} triangular Hopf algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+groups"} quantum groups
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hopfish+algebras"} hopfish algebras
            etc. Most of these notions are systematized via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            by the properties and structures on the coresponding
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
            %em
              %a{:href => "#TannakaDuality"} Tannaka duality
            below.
          %h2#definition Definition
          #Antipode.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antipode"}> antipode
                )
            %p
              A
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra"} bialgebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
