          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              %strong Caution: convention in topology
            %p
              In
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+object"} graded
              and “connected” in the sense that its degree-0 part is just the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
              (a property possessed by the homology or cohomology of any connected space). In algebraic topology also the strict coassociativity is not always taken for granted.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_hopf_groups Relation to Hopf groups
          %p
            Note that the definition of Hopf algebra (or, really, of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+monoid"} Hopf monoid
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} self-dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is the same as a Hopf monoid in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^{op}
            (i.e. a “Hopf comonoid”). Thus we can view a Hopf algebra as “like a group” in two different ways, depending on whether the group multiplication corresponds to the multiplication or the comultiplication of the Hopf algebra. The formal connections between Hopf monoids and group objects are:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                A Hopf monoid in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is the same as a group object in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %em cocommutative
                (that is, their underlying comonoid is cocommutative). This is because every object of a cartesian monoidal category is a cocommutative comonoid object in a unique way, and every morphism is a comonoid homomorphism.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %em commutative
                Hopf monoid in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is the same as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi CMon
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CMon(V)^{op}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CMon
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CMon(V)
                is the category of commutative monoids in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cogroup"} cogroup
                object in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CMon
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CMon(V)
                (a point highlighted by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haynes+Miller"} Haynes Miller
                = succeed "))." do
                  %a{:href => "#Ravenel86"} Ravenel 86, appendix A1
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                in its
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+Hopf+algebra"} commutative Hopf algebra
                is the same as a group object in the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Alg
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg^{op}
                of affine schemes.
          %p
            Corresponding to these two, an ordinary group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            gives us two different Hopf algebras (here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is the
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+ring"} ground ring
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[G]
                (the free vector space on the set
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                and comultiplication given by the diagonal
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi g
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\mapsto g\otimes g
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is abelian. It can be viewed as the result of applying the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} strong monoidal functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo :
                      %mi Set
                      %mo →
                      %mi k
                      %mi Mod
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[-]:Set \to k Mod
                to the Hopf monoid
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %li
              %p
                The function algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(G)
                (the set of functions
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mo →
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\to k
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo ×
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(G) \to k(G\times G) \cong k(G)\otimes k(G),
              %p
                and multiplication given by pointwise multiplication in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                in order to guarantee
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi G
                        %mo ×
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(G\times G) \cong k(G)\otimes k(G)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is abelian.
          %p
            Note that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
