          %h3#as_3vector_spaces As 3-vector spaces
          %p
            A Hopf algebra structure on an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            canonically defines on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            the structure of an algebra object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of algebras,
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
          %p
            By the discussion at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-vector+space"} n-vector space
            this allows to identify Hopf algebras with certain
            %em 3-vector spaces
            \.
          %p
            (For instance (
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups"}> FHLT, p. 27
            )).
          %p
            More general 3-vector spaces are given by
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hopfish+algebras"} hopfish algebras
            and generally by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sesquiunital+sesquialgebras"} sesquiunital sesquialgebras
          %h3#relation_to_frobenius_algebras Relation to Frobenius algebras
          %p
            Both Hopf algebras and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebras"} Frobenius algebras
            are at the same time an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            and a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#LS69"} Larson &amp; Sweedler (1969)
          #HopfAlgebraIsFrobeniusAlgebra.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
              Hopf algebra can be endowed with the structure of a Frobenius algebra.
          %p
            The finite-dimensional condition is expected since all Frobenius algebras are finite-dimensional. A nontrivial part of this result is that, while a finite-dimensional Hopf algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            already has a counit, this is not be the same counit that realizes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as a Frobenius algebra. Rather, it is an
            %em integral
            (see
            %a{:href => "#LS69"} Larson &amp; Sweedler (1969)
            for details on this), which for finite-dimensional Hopf algebras always exist and are unique up to scaling.
          %p
            In fact, more is true. In
            %a{:href => "#FSS11"} Fuchs, Schweigert &amp; Stigner (2011)
            a
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra#special_frobenius_algebras"} symmetric special Frobenius algebra
            is constructed from a particular kind of Hopf algebra.
          #DualHopfAlgebraIsSSFrobeniusAlgebra.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              be a finite-dimensional factorizable ribbon Hopf algebra, with multiplication
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
              be a left-integral element of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
              a right-cointegral of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              (since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              is finite-dimensional, this is equivalent to an integral of the dual Hopf algebra
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^*
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi Λ
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda\circ\Lambda=1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^*
              endowed with: a unit
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon^*
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^*
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^*
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mtext id
                      %mi H
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mtext id
                      %mi H
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mtext id
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mtext id
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_F=((\text{id}_H\otimes (\lambda\circ \mu))\circ (\text{id}_H\otimes S\otimes\text{id}_H)\circ (\Delta\otimes\text{id}_H))^*
            %p
              is a symmetric Frobenius object in the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext Bimod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Bimod}(H,H)
              of bimodules of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              is is semisimple.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steenrod+algebra"} Steenrod algebra
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+type"} finite type
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H-space"} H-space
                (for instance a
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/based+loop+space"} based loop space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
                via its
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin+ring"} Pontrjagin ring
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} structure
                – see at
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+of+loop+spaces"} homology of loop spaces
                for more
          %h3#extensions Extensions
          %p
            As in the case of groups, more interesting of Hopf algebras can be constructed using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+extension"} extensions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{C} \to A \to C \to B \to \mathbb{C},
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C},A,B,C
            are Hopf algebras,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to C
            is injective,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi:C\to B
            is surjective, the composition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            factors through the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-unitality"} counit
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ker
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ker(B\to C)
            is the augmentation ideal of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi π
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mtext id
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A:= \{ x \in C \vert (\pi\otimes \text{id})\Delta(x) = 1\otimes x\}
          %p
            Such extensions are classified by extensions data
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi τ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\rho',\rho,\sigma,\tau)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho': B\otimes A \to A
            a weak action,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho: B\to B\otimes A
            a weak coaction,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma: B\otimes B \to A
            a twisted 2-cocycle, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau: B \to A \otimes A
            a twisted dual 2-cocycle, satisfying compatibility conditions (see (
            %a{:href => "#AD93"}> Andruskiewitsch and Devoto 93
            )).
          %p
            The complexity of this data reduces considerably when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A= (\mathbb{C}[\Gamma])^*
            is a dual group algebra, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B= \mathbb{C}[G]
            is a group algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{C} \to (\mathbb{C}[\Gamma])^* \to C \to \mathbb{C}[G] \to \mathbb{C},
          %p
            as the extensions data becomes a pair of weak actions and a pair of twisted 2-cocycles which, nevertheless, can still give rise to non-commutative and non-cocommutative Hopf algebras. See (
            %a{:href => "#Tak81"}> Takeuchi 81
            ) and (
            %a{:href => "#Mas02"}> Masuoka 02
            ) for more.
          %h3#the_kacpaljutkin__hopf_algebra
            The Kac-Paljutkin
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 8
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_8
            Hopf algebra
          %p
            The Kac-Paljutkin
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 8
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_8
            Hopf algebra was first described in
            %a{:href => "#KP66"} Kac &amp; Paljutkin (1966)
            and it is the Hopf algebra with the smallest dimension that is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+object"} semisimple
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} noncommutative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocommutative+coalgebra"} noncocommutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+object"} self-dual
            (see e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Burciu17"} Burciu (2017)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x,y,z\}
            satisfying the relations
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^2=y^2=z^2=1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi xz
                    %mo =
                    %mi zx
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} xz=zx
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi zy
                    %mo =
                    %mi yz
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} zy=yz
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi xyz
                    %mo =
                    %mi yx
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} xyz=yx
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi xe
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi xe
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(x)=xe_0\otimes x+xe_1\otimes y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ye
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi ye
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(y)=ye_1\otimes x+ye_0\otimes y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi z
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(z)=z\otimes z
          %p
            (where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_0=\frac{1}{2}(1+z)
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_1=\frac{1}{2}(1-z)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon(x)=\epsilon(y)=\epsilon(z)=1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi xe
                    %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi ye
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(x)=xe_0+ye_1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi xe
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi ye
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(y)=xe_1+ye_0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(z)=z
          %p
            \. Its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+representations"} category of representations
            is the unique
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2\times\mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tambara-Yamagami+category"} Tambara-Yamagami category
            that admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+functor"} fiber functor
            and is not the representation category of some group.
          %h3#generalized_kacpaljutkin_algebras Generalized Kac-Paljutkin algebras
          %p
            There exist some generalizations of the Kac-Paljutkin algebras. For instance, in (
            %a{:href => "#Pan17"}> Pansera 17
            ), a
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n^2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %msup
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\mathbb{C}[\mathbb{Z}_n \times \mathbb{Z}_n ])^* \to H_{2n^2 } \to \mathbb{C}[\mathbb{Z}_2 ] .
          %p
            is constructed as an abelian extension (in the sense of (
            %a{:href => "#Tak81"}> Takeuchi 81
            ) and (
            %a{:href => "#Mas02"}> Masuoka 02
            )). The original Kac-Paljutkin algebra corresponds to the case
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
          %p
            A further generalization, a family of Hopf algebras of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi m
                  %mi m
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^m m!
            is constructed in (
            %a{:href => "#Lomp25"}> Lomp 25
            ).
